Experiment 5 — Spring Boot Testing for a Containerized Application
Step 1: Check the required software
Open Ubuntu and run:
java -version
mvn -version
docker --version
docker ps


If Docker isn't running, start Docker Desktop in Windows and ensure its Ubuntu/WSL integration is enabled.
Step 2: Create a new Spring Boot project
Create a separate folder:
mkdir -p ~/Projects/devopsexp/springboot-exp5
cd ~/Projects/devopsexp/springboot-exp5


Create the project structure:
mkdir -p src/main/java/com/example/demo
mkdir -p src/test/java/com/example/demo


Step 3: Create pom.xml
Run:
nano pom.xml


Paste the following Maven configuration:

<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <parent>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-parent</artifactId>
        <version>3.4.5</version>
        <relativePath/>
    </parent>

    <groupId>com.example</groupId>
    <artifactId>springboot-exp5</artifactId>
    <version>0.0.1-SNAPSHOT</version>
    <name>springboot-exp5</name>

    <properties>
        <java.version>21</java.version>
    </properties>

    <dependencies>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-test</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
                <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>
</project>



Save using Ctrl + O, Enter, and Ctrl + X.
Step 4: Create the main Spring Boot application
nano src/main/java/com/example/demo/DemoApplication.java


Paste:
package com.example.demo;

import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;

@SpringBootApplication
public class DemoApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(DemoApplication.class, args);
    }
}


Save and exit.
Step 5: Create a REST controller
nano src/main/java/com/example/demo/HelloController.java


Paste:
package com.example.demo;

import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestParam;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;

@RestController
public class HelloController {

    @GetMapping("/")
    public String home() {
        return "Spring Boot application is running!";
    }

    @GetMapping("/add")
    public int add(@RequestParam int a, @RequestParam int b) {
        return a + b;
    }
}


This application provides two endpoints:
- / — displays a welcome message.
- /add?a=10&b=20 — returns the sum, 30.
Step 6: Write the Spring Boot test cases
Create the test file:
nano src/test/java/com/example/demo/HelloControllerTest.java


Paste:
package com.example.demo;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.boot.test.autoconfigure.web.servlet.AutoConfigureMockMvc;
import org.springframework.boot.test.context.SpringBootTest;
import org.springframework.test.web.servlet.MockMvc;

import static org.springframework.test.web.servlet.request.MockMvcRequestBuilders.get;
import static org.springframework.test.web.servlet.result.MockMvcResultMatchers.content;
import static org.springframework.test.web.servlet.result.MockMvcResultMatchers.status;

@SpringBootTest
@AutoConfigureMockMvc
class HelloControllerTest {

    @Autowired
    private MockMvc mockMvc;

    @Test
    void testHome() throws Exception {
        mockMvc.perform(get("/"))
                .andExpect(status().isOk())
                .andExpect(content().string(
                        "Spring Boot application is running!"));
    }

    @Test
    void testAddition() throws Exception {
        mockMvc.perform(get("/add")
                        .param("a", "10")
                        .param("b", "20"))
                .andExpect(status().isOk())
                .andExpect(content().string("30"));
    }
}


Save and exit.
These tests verify the HTTP status and the response returned by each endpoint.
Step 7: Execute the test cases
From the project directory, run:
mvn clean test


Wait for Maven to download dependencies and execute the tests.
Expected result:
Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0, Skipped: 0

BUILD SUCCESS


The exact output may vary. Do not proceed until the tests pass.
Step 8: Package the application
mvn clean package


Verify that the JAR file exists:
ls target/*.jar


Step 9: Create the Dockerfile
nano Dockerfile


Paste:
FROM eclipse-temurin:21-jre
WORKDIR /app
COPY target/springboot-exp5-0.0.1-SNAPSHOT.jar app.jar
EXPOSE 8080
ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar", "app.jar"]


Save and exit.
Step 10: Build the Docker image
Ensure Docker Desktop is running and then execute:
docker build -t springboot-exp5 .


Verify:
docker images


You should see an image named springboot-exp5.
Step 11: Run the container
Run the application on host port 8081:
docker run -d --name springboot-exp5-container -p 8081:8080 springboot-exp5


Check the container:
docker ps


View its startup logs:
docker logs springboot-exp5-container


Wait until the logs indicate that Spring Boot has started.
If the container name is already in use from an earlier attempt, remove the old container first:
docker rm -f springboot-exp5-container


If port 8081 is busy, use another host port, such as 8082, with -p 8082:8080.
Step 12: Test the running container
Run these commands from Ubuntu:
curl http://localhost:8081/


Expected output:
Spring Boot application is running!


Test addition:
curl "http://localhost:8081/add?a=10&b=20"


Expected output:
30


You can also open these URLs in your Windows browser:
- http://localhost:8081/
- http://localhost:8081/add?a=10&b=20
Step 13: Stop the container
After verification:
docker stop springboot-exp5-container


To remove it later:
docker rm springboot-exp5-contain
